:issue: <br>
= contingent a posteriori and necessary a priori =
====== short ======
examples of the cases. An explanation of how they arise, and consideration of
their relationship to indexicals/indexical like structures (variation of truth
conditions due to different context of utterance or evaluation).

====== related to ======
[[kripke1980-namingAndNecessity]]++, [[putnam1975-meaningOfMeaning]]++

++[[local:zz.texts/williamson1986-theContingentAPrioriHasItAnythingToDoWithIndexicals.pdf]]
-> [[local:zz.texts/williamson11988-theContingentAPrioriAReply.pdf]] (a defence of the previous paper against a particular attack)

== necessary a posteriori ==
    All water is H2O.

This statement, made in some contexts of utterance, is true in every possible
world.
Meaning ~= mapping (context of utterance) -> (truth conditions).
In this possible world (the real world) the truth conditions that sentence is
mapped to is the set of all possible worlds.

There is no possible world in which water is not H,,2,,O. But there are possible
worlds in which the term <q>water</q> (by people in some sense speaking the same
language as us) denotes a different natural kind.

In a world where the potable, transparent, wet stuff <q>around here</q> is XYZ
and not water then <q>All water is H,,2,,O</q> is false. (Its truth conditions,
as uttered in that possible world, are the empty set.)

== contingent a priori ==
Kripke's most famous example (from [[kripke1980-namingAndNecessity|_Naming and Necessity_]]) is:
    The standard metre bar is one metre long at t₀.

another example is:
    I am here.

In each (or at least many) world/context of utterance in which these statements
are uttered their truth conditions are not all possible worlds.
However, in each world/context in which it is uttered it is true.

Another example in which is generally explained as turning on an indexical
element, but in which the indexical is not overt:
    'If anyone uniquely invented the zip, Julius did', where by stipulation
    'Julius' rigidly designates the inventor of the zip (if there is one)
    (from Davies 1981 via [[local:zz.texts/williamson1986-theContingentAPrioriHasItAnythingToDoWithIndexicals.pdf]])


Rigid designators give rise to the same issues as indexicals since which object
is designated by a rigid designator depends upon the world in which the name is
bestowed.

=== non-indexical examples ===
Williamson's non-indexical example is:
    There is at least one believer.
and claims that this is knowable _a priori_ because
This is *self-evident* but not necessary.

This is silly. It is true in every world in which it is uttered but not true in
every possible world. I can know it on the basis of my own existence (a la the
[[descartes#cogito|cogito]]) and hence be certain of it.


